  {
   "id": "Fl6U7HqaAwAJ",
   "from": "Daniel Turek",
   "author": "c9e9cbbfb3b043f0",
   "date": "2022-10-19T10:53:48Z",
   "subject": "Re: Using Nimble fitting a locally smooth model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Alex, some quick thoughts on this, although I don&#39;t believe I&#39;m fully grasping your model:<div><br></div><div>A Uniform prior with support on (-Inf, Inf), can be achieved using the dflat() prior distribution.</div><div><br></div><div>That said, the Uniform prior specification for your beta_i coefficients, each of which depends on beta_{I+1} and beta_{i-1}, would cause a cyclic graph definition, which is not allowed in NIMBLE.  There may be other ways to achieve what you&#39;re trying to do here, mathematically identical, perhaps with a multivariate specification of the full beta vector.</div><div><br></div><div>Yes, the multinomial prior can be used in NIMBLE, that won&#39;t pose a problem.  If your multinomial has 1 trial, I&#39;ll point out that&#39;s equivalent to using a categorical prior (dcat), which will be sampled in a fundamentally different way, and I suspect would improve the overall mixing of your model.</div><div><br></div><div>Others may have more helpful advice for you, but these were my quick thoughts.</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Daniel</div><div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, Oct 18, 2022 at 11:00 PM Alex Lu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">yangxu...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
