  {
   "id": "Ve1-40xODQAJ",
   "from": "Chris Paciorek",
   "author": "d78486bfff620d81",
   "date": "2022-10-20T01:46:06Z",
   "subject": "Re: Using Nimble fitting a locally smooth model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi Alex,<div><br></div><div>I&#39;ll add some thoughts to Daniel&#39;s suggestions:</div><div><br></div><div>1) Following Daniel&#39;s comment about the betas, the difficulty here is specifying the joint distribution either directly as a joint distribution or as the product of a marginal and various conditional</div><div>distributions, without having any cycles. Alternatively, you might be able to specify the beta_i values as being independent of each other and use our `dconstraint` mechanism to impose the monotonicity constraints you want. Another option would be to put your prior on the differences in the betas. I.e., you could have a prior for beta1 that has fixed constants as parameters and have beta2 = beta1 + delta1, beta3 = beta2 + delta2, etc., with the priors put on the deltas (and enforcing that each delta must be non-negative). This is appealing in terms of model specification but it might lead to slow inference because a given observation can depend on many of the deltas because of the dependency structure that such a specification produces.</div><div><br></div><div>2) Your specification for phi looks like a CAR prior. Is that right? We do provide both proper and improper CAR distributions in NIMBLE.</div><div><br></div><div>chris</div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Wed, Oct 19, 2022 at 3:53 AM Daniel Turek &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">db...@williams.edu</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Alex, some quick thoughts on this, although I don&#39;t believe I&#39;m fully grasping your model:<div><br></div><div>A Uniform prior with support on (-Inf, Inf), can be achieved using the dflat() prior distribution.</div><div><br></div><div>That said, the Uniform prior specification for your beta_i coefficients, each of which depends on beta_{I+1} and beta_{i-1}, would cause a cyclic graph definition, which is not allowed in NIMBLE.  There may be other ways to achieve what you&#39;re trying to do here, mathematically identical, perhaps with a multivariate specification of the full beta vector.</div><div><br></div><div>Yes, the multinomial prior can be used in NIMBLE, that won&#39;t pose a problem.  If your multinomial has 1 trial, I&#39;ll point out that&#39;s equivalent to using a categorical prior (dcat), which will be sampled in a fundamentally different way, and I suspect would improve the overall mixing of your model.</div><div><br></div><div>Others may have more helpful advice for you, but these were my quick thoughts.</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Daniel</div><div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Tue, Oct 18, 2022 at 11:00 PM Alex Lu &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">yangxu...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br>Hi Nimble experts,<div><br></div><div>I am trying to fit a locally smooth model, and I am wondering if this can be achieved in Nimble. The general idea of this model is to allow a large jump in the true values between adjacent areal units. Please see the attached picture for model description. Specifically, I am not sure if nimble allows the uniform prior has (-inf, inf). Thanks in advance!</div><div><br></div><div>The beta is a piecewise constant intercept that allows sudden jumps in the spatial surface between adjacent units. This constant intercept term has q distinct values, and if adjacent units are in different groups, their probability may have different values. Additionally, the number 1 before the semi-colon in the multinomial model indicates there is only &#39;one&#39; trail in the multinomial distribution, as each areal unit can only have one intercept term. I am also wondering if the multinomial prior can be achieved under Nimble. </div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/nimble-users/attach/d4e4ce37eed55/Model.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"Model.png\" width=\"243\" height=\"142\" style=\"margin-right:0px\"><br></div><div><br></div><div>Many thanks</div><div>Best,</div><div>Alex</div><div><br></div><div><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\">Y</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\"> </span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\">(1)</span><br role=\"presentation\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:Lato,sans-serif;font-size:16px\"><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\">i</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\"> </span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\">∼</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\"> </span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\">Binomial</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\">(</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\">N</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\"> </span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\">(1)</span><br role=\"presentation\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:Lato,sans-serif;font-size:16px\"><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\">i</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\"> </span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:11.0371px;font-family:sans-serif\">, p</span><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-size:7.35802px;font-family:sans-serif\">(1)</span><br role=\"presentation\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:transparent;white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:Lato,sans-serif;font-size:16px\"><span role=\"presentation\" dir=\"ltr\" style=\"box-sizing:border-box